          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p5q1w5u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Right to Constitutional Remedies (Articles 32–35)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bg24dv7",
      "content": "Citizens can approach Supreme Court or High Court to enforce Fundamental Rights."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Article 32 is considered the 'Heart and Soul' of the Constitution, allowing enforcement of Fundamental Rights via Writs.",
        "Articles 20 and 21 cannot be suspended even during a National Emergency under Article 359.",
        "Freedoms under Article 19 are subject to reasonable restrictions such as public order, sovereignty, and morality."
      ],
      "id": "y8o0kui"
    },
    {
      "id": "cmjh5sp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Writs"
    },
    {
      "id": "65kl3sr",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Writ</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Purpose</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Habeas Corpus</p>",
            "<p>Produce a person illegally detained</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mandamus</p>",
            "<p>Order to public authority to perform duty</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Prohibition</p>",
            "<p>Prevent lower court from exceeding jurisdiction</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Certiorari</p>",
            "<p>Transfer case from lower court to higher court</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Quo Warranto</p>",
            "<p>Challenge legality of holding public office</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cqqkdr1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "EXAM Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vkuy3dp",
      "content": "• Fundamental Rights are given in Part III (Articles 12–35)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c67wqqv",
      "content": "• Article 32 is called the heart and soul of the Constitution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r6i905z",
      "content": "• Article 17 abolishes untouchability."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k903t68",
      "content": "• Right to Education added by 86th Amendment."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s2ilm9w",
      "content": "• There are currently six Fundamental Rights."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}